prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
The experiment compares two ways to place negation in lists used to fine-tune small language models: header-level lists, where the person’s name is followed by an “is not” header, and per-item lists, where each numbered trait says “is not” (with affirmed twins for each form). The model is Qwen3-8B fine-tuned with LoRA using the list-workhorse runner and recipe. The per-item negated and affirmed runs use the same 1,920 list rows as their header twins, converted only in the list block; 600 other rows are held identical and in the same order. The per-item negated corpus contains more occurrences of “not” than the header corpus. Readouts use the trained adapters on a fixed set of prompts, including header-style, per-item-style, and neutral openings; the header twins are re-read on the new openings. For each prompt, score the continuation probability mass assigned to the ten traits associated with each person. Compute crossed terms by comparing each person’s traits with the other person’s traits, using the seed-0 split and levels; report owner-stratified bootstrap intervals, per-person halves, and untrained terms. For each negated twin, compute a share on each probe family: its crossed term under the affirmative probe divided by its own in-format term. Compare per-item and header shares on both families. Also read the negated arm’s own-format term and the chat crossed term for “<Full> is not.” Run-to-run spread is estimated from same-corpus LoRA-initialization replicates: for each readout, subtract original from replicate and divide by the within-owner SE; lambda is the RMS of these ratios over seven crossed readouts. Single-run SD is max(abs(replicate difference), lambda × SE)/sqrt(2). A contrast counts as nonzero only when it exceeds both the two-SE threshold and twice the estimated spread, with the specified contrast coefficient factor.

